<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chlodwig zu Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Chlodwig, Prince of Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chlodwig zu Hohenlohe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schillingsfurst">Schillingsfürst</a> (1819–1901) was a German statesman who served as prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1866 to 1870, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a> from 1885 and Chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and Prussian prime minister from 1894 to 1900. A liberal Catholic aristocrat, he backed German unification under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, but as chancellor he was largely overshadowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chlodwig-zu-hohenlohe-schillingsfurst.jpg" alt="Chlodwig zu Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: various · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Die_Gartenlaube_(1894)_b_773_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chlodwig Carl Viktor of Hohenlohe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schillingsfurst">Schillingsfürst</a> was born on 31 March 1819 at Rotenburg an der Fulda, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, the second of six sons. His father was a Catholic and his mother a Lutheran, and the sons were brought up as Catholics according to the custom of the time. As the younger son of a cadet line he needed a profession. He considered a commission in the British army through his aunt, a half-sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, but chose the Prussian civil service. The years of training confirmed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>: he favoured a free press and German unity under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, but opposed the Prussification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866 he argued for closer union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. On 31 December 1866 he was appointed minister of the royal house and of foreign affairs and president of the council of ministers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, reportedly at the urging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>. He reorganised the Bavarian army on the Prussian model, brought about a military union of the southern states and became a vice-president of the customs parliament in 1868. He opposed the ultramontane party over the First Vatican Council, and the combination of ultramontanes and Bavarian patriots forced him to resign on 7 March 1870.</p><p>He was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in 1871 and became its vice-president. He helped found the Imperial Liberal Party and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Bismarck</a>'s Kulturkampf. Bismarck made him ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1873, and in 1880 he served briefly as head of the Foreign Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a> from 1885 he carried out the coercive measures of 1887 and 1888, though he largely disapproved of them, and his conciliatory manner did much to reconcile the population to German rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In October 1894 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>, at his urgent request, made the aged Hohenlohe Imperial Chancellor in succession to Leo von Caprivi. He rarely appeared in the Prussian or German parliaments and left great independence to his secretaries of state, among them Bernhard von Bülow. Wilhelm's reshuffles of 1897, which brought Bülow to the Foreign Office and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-von-tirpitz">Alfred von Tirpitz</a> to the Naval Office, in effect disempowered him.</p><p>He could not halt the turn to imperialist world policy and naval armament. He attempted a reform of the Prussian Military Law in 1898 and a Law on Associations in 1899, and the Bürgerliches Gesetzbuch was adopted in 1896. He resigned on 17 October 1900, was succeeded by Bülow, and died on 6 July 1901 at Bad Ragaz, aged 82.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chlodwig, Prince of Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The family remained prominent: <b>Chlodwig zu Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst</b> served as German Chancellor from 1894 to 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-hohenlohe">County of Hohenlohe</a></li><li><blockquote>The family remained prominent: <b>Chlodwig zu Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst</b> was Bavarian prime minister and German chancellor from 1894 to 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hohenlohe-schillingsfurst">Principality of Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rotenburg an der Fulda, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Bad Ragaz, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51,9.73&amp;year=1872" aria-label="Rotenburg an der Fulda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4771 395 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chlodwig zu Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5221 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5221 665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51,9.73&amp;year=1872">What was here in 1872? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
